Q: What is the difference between wait() and sleep()?

📌 Real-life Analogy:
sleep(): Aap ne alarm lagaya aur so gaye — kisi ko bataya nahi.
wait(): Aap kehte ho: "Mujhe uthana jab kaam ho jaye" — jab tak koi notify na kare.

One line:
sleep() = timed pause (keeps lock). wait() = wait for signal (releases lock; needs synchronized).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Thread.sleep(ms)

Defined in: java.lang.Thread (static method).

What it does:
- Puts the **current** thread to sleep for given time
- Does NOT release any monitor/lock it already holds
- After time expires (or interrupt) → becomes Runnable again

Usage:
Thread.sleep(2000); // sleep current thread 2 seconds

Checked Exception: InterruptedException

👉 Use when you only want a timed delay.

Interview trap:
someThread.sleep(1000) still sleeps the **current** thread (sleep is static).
Always write Thread.sleep(...).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Object.wait() / wait(ms)

Defined in: java.lang.Object (every object has a monitor).

What it does:
- Current thread waits until:
  - another thread calls notify() / notifyAll() on the **same** object, OR
  - optional timeout expires
- Releases the monitor/lock on that object while waiting
- Before continuing after wake-up, it must **re-acquire** the same lock

Usage (MUST own the monitor):
synchronized (lock) {
    lock.wait(); // releases lock, waits, then re-acquires before returning
}

Without synchronized → IllegalMonitorStateException

Checked Exception: InterruptedException

👉 Use for inter-thread communication (producer-consumer).

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ CONFUSION CLEAR: In which scenario does wait() release the lock?

ONLY this scenario:
Thread is INSIDE synchronized, then calls wait() on that same lock object.

What happens step by step:

synchronized (lock) {          // 1) Thread-A TAKES the lock
    while (!ready) {
        lock.wait();           // 2) Thread-A RELEASES the lock here and sleeps
                               //    (so Thread-B can enter synchronized)
    }
    // 3) After notify: Thread-A wakes, RE-TAKES the lock, then continues
    doWork();
}                              // 4) Leaving synchronized → lock released normally

Timeline example (Shared object lock):

Time  Event
----  -----
T1    Consumer enters synchronized → Consumer HOLDS lock
T2    available == false → consumer calls wait()
      → Consumer RELEASES lock and waits   ★ this is the release scenario
T3    Producer enters synchronized (possible only because consumer released)
T4    Producer sets available=true, calls notify()
T5    Consumer is notified, but must wait to get lock again
T6    Producer exits synchronized → releases lock
T7    Consumer RE-ACQUIRES lock → exits wait() → continues consume()

So:
- wait() releases lock → YES, immediately when wait() is called (while waiting)
- sleep() inside synchronized → NO, lock stays with the sleeping thread

Compare side by side:

synchronized (lock) {
    Thread.sleep(5000); // holds lock 5 sec → OTHER threads BLOCKED on this lock
}

synchronized (lock) {
    lock.wait();        // releases lock → OTHER threads CAN enter
}

Rule:
wait() release = “I am waiting for a condition, so I give the lock away until someone signals me.”

────────────────────────────────────────
🔑 Main Differences

| Feature                    | sleep()                         | wait()                                      |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Belongs to                 | Thread class                    | Object class                                |
| Lock behavior              | Does **not** release lock       | **Releases** lock while waiting             |
| synchronized required?     | No                              | **Yes** (must own monitor)                  |
| Wakes up by                | time / interrupt                | notify / notifyAll / timeout / interrupt    |
| Purpose                    | timed pause                     | thread coordination                         |
| Which thread               | always current thread           | current thread waiting on that object       |

────────────────────────────────────────
Example (Producer-Consumer) — while, not if

class Shared {
    private boolean available = false;

    public synchronized void produce() throws InterruptedException {
        while (available) {   // while protects against spurious wakeup
            wait();
        }
        System.out.println("Produced");
        available = true;
        notify(); // or notifyAll() if multiple waiters
    }

    public synchronized void consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (!available) {
            wait();
        }
        System.out.println("Consumed");
        available = false;
        notify();
    }
}

Why while (not if)?
Spurious wakeup / multiple waiters — always re-check the condition after wait() returns.

notify() vs notifyAll():
- notify()     → wakes one waiting thread
- notifyAll()  → wakes all; they compete for the lock (safer when unsure)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ In short
- sleep() → delay only; lock stays held
- wait()  → communicate; lock released; must be in synchronized

✅ 30-second answer
“sleep is Thread’s timed pause and does not release locks. wait is Object’s coordination API: call it inside synchronized, it releases the monitor until notify/notifyAll/timeout,
then re-acquires the lock. Use sleep for delay, wait/notify for producer-consumer style signaling.”
